From 0fd8611f5b349feb3ff044689170fa2db20705ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:19:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] agentHost: hydrate Claude replay attribution on a live
 session

The replay attribution index landed as foundation only: nothing called
hydrateAttribution, so a restored session started every turn with an
empty tool-call registry. A tool_result for a tool_use announced before
the restart hit the unknown tool_use_id warning path, and once the
registry was hydrated it would instead be stolen by the in-flight turn.

Three parts.

Hydrate on resume. The mapper is synchronous, so the seed has to be
complete before the first SDK message is mapped. The session reads the
transcript at materialize when ctx.isResume is set and hands the
resulting promise to the pipeline, which forwards it to the router; the
router awaits it once at the top of handle, which the consumer loop
already awaits. The read is not awaited inside materialize, so it
overlaps the rest of startup rather than adding transcript-sized latency
to the first send. The design note suggested deriving the seed from the
read the restore already performs, but that read is
ClaudeAgent._reconstructTurns, driven by the renderer asking for a
transcript: it can run before the session exists, after it, or never, so
hydration would have depended on the renderer. The seed gets its own
read; the awaiting seam is unchanged.

Consult it in the mapper. mapUserMessage now takes the in-flight turnId.
A tool_result whose entry is hydrated and belongs to a different turn is
logged against its committed turn, dropped from the registry, and emits
no completion action: the reducer only mutates the active turn, so a
completion aimed at a committed one is discarded downstream anyway.
Live entries are untouched, so a steering preempt still completes its
tool on its own turn, and an unknown id still warns as before.

Drain on rebind. abort() never delivers a result, so the per-turn
cleanup that runs on a final result never runs for an aborted turn and
its entries were still present when the next turn ended. The pipeline
now drains at the point it resets for a rebind. The drain lives on a new
router method rather than back in mapResult, matching the shape
fix/keep-tool-attribution-across-steer moves that cleanup to.

Replayed history is guarded separately: the SDK marks re-emitted
transcript envelopes with isReplay on SDKUserMessageReplay, so those are
dropped before any attribution rather than being treated as live
tool_results.
