+ * The `isReplay` twin of {@link makeUserToolResultMessage}: transcript history the
+ * SDK re-emits on resume, which must never be re-attributed as a live tool_result.
+ */
+export function makeReplayedUserToolResultMessage(
+	sessionId: string,
+	toolUseId: string,
+	content: string,
+): SDKUserMessageReplay {
+	return {
+		type: 'user',
+		message: {
+			role: 'user',
+			content: [{ type: 'tool_result', tool_use_id: toolUseId, content }],
+		},
+		parent_tool_use_id: null,
+		isSynthetic: true,
+		uuid: TEST_UUID,
+		session_id: sessionId,
+		isReplay: true,
+	};
+}

From db2a348f54c99ae6fd08f2adaebd7c65899999a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:44:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] agentHost: drain replay attribution only when a rebind
 replaced a dead query

Two review findings on the live hydration.

The rebind drain was unconditional, but _rebindQuery also serves healthy
'restart' rebinds for tool, plugin and MCP synchronization, including the
pre-flight before a resumed session's first send. Clearing there erased
an already-hydrated seed before any turn boundary, so whether a late
tool_result kept its committed-turn attribution depended on whether the
transcript read had resolved before or after that line. That is the
timing dependence hydration exists to remove.

The discriminator is _needsRebind, read at the drain point. `reason`
only records why the caller asked, and it is not reliable: the session's
send pre-flight calls rebindForRestart before pipeline.send, which is
what would otherwise have taken the 'recover' branch, so a rebind that
genuinely replaced an aborted query can arrive with reason 'restart'.
_needsRebind is the pipeline's own record of the replaced query's
health, set by abort() and by the consumer loop's catch for cancellation
or crash, and cleared only after a successful rebuild. Reading it at the
drain rather than capturing it on entry also covers a crash that lands
while the rematerializer is still awaiting.

The isReplay guard sat inside mapUserMessage, but the router runs the
file-edit observer ahead of the mapper, so a replayed envelope was still
observed as if live and its pre-restart edits could be attributed to the
resumed turn. The test is now in the router, once, before both the
observer and the mapper, with the mapper keeping its own guard as
defence in depth through a shared isReplayedUserMessage predicate so the
two cannot drift. Nothing in flight touches claudeFileEditObserver.ts,
so the earlier note about colliding observer work was wrong.

The agent-level restart-rebind test that came with the first round is
removed rather than repaired: materialize consumes every send pre-flight
trigger, so no agent-surface lever reaches a restart rebind while the
seed is still live, and the test passed either way. The discriminator is
covered at the pipeline seam instead, where both directions are driven
directly.
